ARLIKAT - MARKETING PLAN, COMPETITIVE LANDSCAPE AND WEBSITE PLAN
Compiled 2026-08-21 - companion to "arlikat-feature-list"


ASSUMPTION THIS PLAN IS BUILT ON
================================

Arlikat is today a client-specific build. This plan assumes the intent
is to productise it and sell it to more companies like Arlikat, under
whatever brand is chosen (Arlikat, or a Blute product name). If the
intent is only to market Arlikat's own services, ignore sections 4-6
and use section 3 and 8 only.

Everything below is grounded in features that exist in the code today.
Section 8 of the feature-list file lists what does NOT exist - none of
it appears as a claim here.


------------------------------------------------------------
1. POSITIONING
------------------------------------------------------------

The one-line positioning

  For companies that sell a machine and then service it for the next
  ten years - one system from the first enquiry to the last AMC
  renewal.

The problem statement customers will recognise

  A machinery dealer runs Tally for accounts, Excel for AMC
  contracts, a CRM trial nobody logs into, and WhatsApp for the
  service team. The machine is sold in one system, installed in a
  second, serviced in a third, and the spare that went out with the
  engineer never comes back to any of them. The AMC that expired last
  month is discovered next quarter. The standby machine sitting at a
  customer site for eight months is on nobody's report.

Why this is a real category gap

  - Horizontal CRMs (Zoho, Salesforce, Freshsales) sell the machine
    and then stop. No installed base, no AMC billing schedule, no
    spare indent.
  - Horizontal ERPs (SAP B1, Business Central, NetSuite, Odoo) handle
    inventory and finance but their field-service modules are either
    expensive add-ons or thin, and implementation runs 6-18 months.
  - FSM specialists (ServiceMax, Zuper, FieldEZ, Zoho FSM) service the
    machine but do not sell it - no quote-with-buyback, no margin
    approval, no purchase and GRN chain.
  - Tally does the books and nothing else.

  Arlikat is the only one of these that owns the whole chain in one
  database: enquiry -> quote (with buyback) -> order -> purchase ->
  GRN with serial number -> dispatch -> installation -> asset ->
  warranty -> AMC -> PM schedule -> complaint -> work order -> spare
  indent -> service bill -> renewal.

The three message pillars (use these in this order, everywhere)

  Pillar 1 - Stop revenue leaking out of your installed base
    Warranty expiries, AMC renewals and service bills due in the next
    30/60/90 days, in quantity and value, on one screen. Non-returned
    standby machines with aging. Estimation vs invoice variance.
    This is the pillar that gets the meeting. Lead with money, not
    features.

  Pillar 2 - One system, sales to service, no re-keying
    The serial number entered once on the GRN is the same serial number
    the engineer consumes a spare against three years later. Info tabs
    on every document show what was created from it.

  Pillar 3 - Built for how Indian equipment companies actually run
    GST, HSN, e-invoice with IRN and QR, e-way bill, multi-branch
    GSTIN, buyback on quotes, margin-gated approvals, spare indent
    against a work order, multi-tenant with per-screen permissions.

Proof points to put on every page (all real, all countable)
  - 4 role dashboards, ~50 report screens, Excel and PDF on all of them
  - Serial and batch traceability from receipt to consumption
  - Warranty / AMC / bill due in 30-60-90 day buckets by value
  - SLA achievement percentage with business-hours-aware clocks
  - Engineer performance and spare consumption per engineer
  - Payables and receivables aging, dead stock, reorder, item ledger

What NOT to say
  Never say "ERP" as the first word. Every prospect has an ERP horror
  story and a Tally licence. Say "runs your sales and service on one
  system" and let them discover the depth in the demo.


