2. TARGET AUDIENCE
------------------------------------------------------------

2.1 Ideal customer profile (primary - go here first)

  Indian equipment dealers, distributors and OEM service arms that
  have an installed base they are contractually obliged to maintain.

  Firmographics
    - Revenue: Rs 25 cr to Rs 500 cr
    - Employees: 50 to 500
    - Field engineers: 10 to 100 (below 10, they cope with Excel;
      above 100, they buy ServiceMax)
    - Branches: 2 to 15, usually multi-state, so multi-GSTIN matters
    - Installed base: 500 to 20,000 machines under warranty or AMC
    - Existing stack: Tally + Excel + WhatsApp, or a half-abandoned
      Zoho/Salesforce deployment

  The qualifying question that sorts everything
    "How many of your customers' machines go out of warranty next
    quarter, and who is chasing those renewals today?"
    If they cannot answer in under a minute, they are a prospect.

2.2 Verticals, ranked by fit

  Tier 1 - buy fastest, pain is sharpest
    1. Medical and diagnostic equipment dealers (analysers, imaging,
       dental, ICU) - AMC-heavy, uptime is contractual, standby units
       everywhere, calibration records demanded by NABH
    2. Printing, imaging and packaging machinery - consumables plus
       AMC plus spares, classic dealer model
    3. CNC, machine tools and industrial automation - high value
       assets, buyback and trade-in is normal, spare lead times long
    4. Lab and analytical instruments - AMC plus calibration plus
       consumables

  Tier 2 - strong fit, longer cycles
    5. HVAC, chillers and cold chain
    6. Diesel gensets and power backup
    7. Material handling (forklifts, cranes, hoists)
    8. Water and waste-water treatment systems
    9. Weighing systems and test instruments
   10. Commercial kitchen and laundry equipment

  Tier 3 - adjacent, opportunistic
   11. Fire and safety systems
   12. IT hardware, AV and security systems integrators
   13. Elevators and escalators (regulated, often has its own systems)
   14. Solar EPC and O&M

2.3 Buying committee - five people, five different pitches

  A. Promoter / MD / Director  (economic buyer, signs)
     Cares about: money leaking, no single version of truth, being
       dependent on two people who "know everything"
     Pitch: renewal leakage, receivables aging, margin visibility
     Fear: another failed software project, staff won't use it
     Kill it with: a 6-week pilot on one branch, one module

  B. Service head / National service manager  (champion, the real user)
     Cares about: SLA breaches, engineer productivity, spares walking
       out, standby machines never coming back, PM schedules slipping
     Pitch: service dashboard, engineer performance and spare
       consumption reports, escalation matrix, non-returned standby
       aging
     This person is your internal seller. Arm them with the ROI
       numbers - they will present it to the MD for you.

  C. Sales head  (co-champion or blocker)
     Cares about: pipeline accuracy, quote turnaround, discount
       control, target tracking
     Pitch: funnel, actual-vs-target, top-10s, margin approval gates,
       quote-to-order conversion, open order risk
     Blocker risk: they already picked a CRM. Do not fight it head-on;
       show that CRM has no installed base and no AMC.

  D. Finance controller / CFO  (gatekeeper)
     Cares about: GST correctness, e-invoice, receivables and payables
       aging, AMC billing schedules actually being billed
     Pitch: compliance built in, billing schedule automation, aging
       reports, "works with your Tally, doesn't replace it"
     Do not overclaim accounting. Say plainly: no general ledger.

  E. IT / systems admin  (technical gate)
     Cares about: deployment, data migration, permissions, backup
     Pitch: per-screen permission mapping, audit log, login history,
       bulk upload for migration, multi-tenant isolation

  F. Field engineer  (adoption risk, not a buyer)
     Cares about: not filling forms twice, working on a phone
     Pitch: EFSR on the phone browser, spare indent in three taps
     If engineers reject it, the deal churns at renewal. Budget demo
     time for them specifically.

2.4 Trigger events - the moments to reach out

  - Opened a new branch or state (multi-GSTIN pain becomes acute)
  - Won a large AMC tender or a government uptime contract
  - Hired a first "Service Head" or "Customer Support Manager"
  - Lost a contract over an SLA penalty
  - Statutory audit or NABH/ISO audit asked for service records
  - Crossed roughly 25 field engineers
  - Owner's son/daughter joined the business (the classic
    digitisation trigger in Indian family businesses)
  - A Zoho or Salesforce deployment quietly stalled


