3. COMPETITIVE LANDSCAPE
------------------------------------------------------------

3.1 The real competitor

  Not a vendor. It is "Tally + Excel + WhatsApp + the guy who
  remembers everything." It is free, it works badly, and it has no
  champion to displace. Roughly 60% of losses will be no-decision,
  not a competitor. Plan messaging against inertia, not against
  Salesforce.

3.2 Tier 1 - horizontal CRM

  Zoho (CRM + Books + Inventory + FSM + One)
    Their strength: price, brand, Indian, huge partner network, and
      Zoho FSM now exists
    Their weakness: you are buying and stitching 4-5 products;
      no installed-base-to-AMC-to-billing-schedule chain; no buyback;
      no margin approval matrix; Zoho FSM is thin on contracts and
      spares; every module you add re-prices the deal
    How we win: "Show me your AMC renewal pipeline by value for the
      next 90 days" and "show me spare consumption per engineer"
    Where we lose: budget under Rs 3 lakh a year, or a company that
      wants email + accounting + CRM in one bill
    Expect: Zoho is in every deal. Never disparage it - concede the
      CRM, own the service.

  Salesforce Sales Cloud + Field Service
    Strength: brand, depth, ecosystem
    Weakness: cost (often 5-10x), 6-12 month implementation, needs a
      consultant permanently, and still no Indian GST/e-invoice
      without a partner app
    How we win: total cost, time-to-live, India compliance native
    Where we lose: MNC subsidiaries with a global mandate

  LeadSquared, Kylas, Freshsales
    Strength: cheap, fast, good for lead management
    Weakness: sales-only. Nothing after the invoice.
    How we win: trivially, on scope. These are not real competitors,
      they are what the sales head bought last year.

3.3 Tier 2 - mid-market ERP

  SAP Business One
    Strength: the "safe" board-level choice, strong inventory/finance
    Weakness: Rs 25-60 lakh+ all-in, 6-12 months, service module is
      basic, every change is a partner change request
    How we win: implementation risk, service depth, cost, and the fact
      that B1's service module cannot run AMC billing schedules or
      spare indents the way an equipment dealer needs
    Where we lose: when finance depth and auditor comfort decides it

  Microsoft Dynamics 365 Business Central
    Strength: Microsoft trust, good finance, Field Service add-on
    Weakness: cost stacks per module, partner-dependent, long cycle
    How we win: same as B1 - time and service depth

  Oracle NetSuite
    Strength: true cloud, strong finance and multi-entity
    Weakness: expensive, India localisation via partners, FSM is weak
    How we win: cost and service; concede finance

  Odoo
    Strength: cheap, modular, huge module library, field service
      module exists
    Weakness: Indian GST and e-invoice depends on community modules
      that break on upgrade; quality varies wildly by partner;
      equipment-specific flows (buyback, AMC price hike, standby
      tracking) need custom development
    How we win: "how much of this is custom code your partner
      maintains?" and upgrade risk
    Where we lose: price-driven deals with a strong local partner
    This is the most dangerous Tier 2 competitor in India right now.

  ERPNext / Frappe
    Strength: open source, free licence, Indian GST support is decent,
      has asset and maintenance modules
    Weakness: needs in-house technical ownership; service/AMC depth is
      shallow compared to what is built here; reporting effort is
      DIY
    How we win: out-of-the-box service depth, ~50 ready reports,
      4 ready dashboards
    Where we lose: companies with an in-house dev team and a
      zero-licence mandate

  Marg, Busy, Focus 9, TallyPrime add-ons
    Strength: entrenched, cheap, accountant-approved
    Weakness: no CRM, no FSM, no dashboards worth the name
    How we win: coexist. Position as "sits on top of your Tally."

3.4 Tier 3 - FSM specialists (the closest real competition)

  ServitiumCRM (India, machine-service focused)
    The single most direct competitor. Same buyer, same verticals,
      similar chain including AMC and spares.
    How we win: we also own the sell side (quote, buyback, margin
      approval, purchase, GRN). They are service-first. Also compete
      on price and on customisation responsiveness.
    Study their pricing and website structure closely before launch.

  FieldEZ (India)
    Strength: India presence, mobile-first, large deployments
    Weakness: service-only, weak on contracts/AMC finance
    How we win: contracts, billing schedules, sell side

  Zuper, Fieldproxy, FieldCircle
    Strength: modern mobile apps, scheduling, cheap entry
    Weakness: no inventory depth, no purchase, no AMC billing
      schedules, no India invoicing depth
    How we win: on scope. Where we lose: on the engineer mobile app -
      theirs is native and offline-capable, ours is a responsive web
      app. Expect to lose deals where field connectivity is the
      dominant pain. This is the #1 product gap to close.

  ServiceMax (PTC), IFS FSM
    Strength: enterprise-grade, deep installed-base management
    Weakness: enterprise pricing, long implementations
    How we win: below 100 engineers, they are not economical

  Zoho FSM
    Covered above. Cheap, improving, thin on contracts.

  Freshdesk / Zendesk / Jira Service Management
    Ticketing only, no asset-to-AMC-to-spare chain. Sometimes present
      as "we already have a helpdesk" - the answer is that a ticket
      is not a work order and a helpdesk does not know a serial
      number.

3.5 Positioning grid to reproduce on the website

  Draw two axes: "sells the machine" (x) and "services the machine"
  (y). Zoho CRM / LeadSquared sit bottom-right. Zuper / FieldEZ /
  ServiceMax sit top-left. SAP B1 / Odoo sit mid-left. Tally sits at
  origin. Arlikat is the only mark in the top-right quadrant. This
  single graphic will do more selling than any feature table.

3.6 Where we lose - state it internally, plan for it

  1. No native offline mobile app for engineers
  2. No customer self-service portal
  3. No WhatsApp notifications
  4. No general ledger, no proven Tally connector
  5. No public API, no integration marketplace
  6. Single-client track record - no logos, no case studies yet
  7. No G2 / Capterra presence, no analyst coverage

  Items 1-3 are product. Items 6-7 are marketing, and are fixable in
  90 days with one good case study and two review-site listings.


