6. WEBSITE AND PAGE PLAN (IN DEPTH)
------------------------------------------------------------

Principle: every page answers one question for one person and ends in
one call to action. Two CTAs only across the whole site - "Book a
30-minute demo" (primary) and "Calculate your AMC leakage"
(secondary, no-commitment).

6.1 Home page  /

  H1: Sell the machine. Service it for ten years. One system.
  Sub: Quotes, orders, stock, installed base, AMC and field service
       for Indian equipment companies - with GST and e-invoice built
       in.
  Sections in order:
    1. Hero with the service dashboard screenshot (real data blurred),
       primary + secondary CTA
    2. The problem, in their words - 4 short cards: "AMC expired,
       nobody knew" / "Spare went out, never came back" / "SLA
       breached, penalty paid" / "Quote in Excel, order in Tally"
    3. The chain graphic - enquiry to renewal as one horizontal
       flow, with the module names underneath. This is the single
       most important asset on the site.
    4. Three pillars (section 1), one row, each linking deeper
    5. The positioning grid from 3.5
    6. Module strip - 5 cards linking to module pages
    7. Numbers strip - 4 dashboards, ~50 reports, serial-level
       traceability, e-invoice ready
    8. Industry strip - 8 vertical logos/links
    9. Case study block
   10. FAQ (6 questions, including "does it replace Tally?" - answer
       honestly, no)
   11. Closing CTA

6.2 Product overview  /platform

  One page that shows the whole chain, then hands off to module pages.
  Include a "day in the life" narrative for three people: the sales
  engineer, the service coordinator, the field engineer. Narrative
  beats feature lists for this buyer.

